# Data Overview

Source: NYC DOHMH Restaurant Inspection Results (file dated 2026-08-27).

## Size and structure
- 296,012 rows and 27 columns.
- Each row is one violation, not one inspection. An inspection with several violations appears on several rows.
- 31,366 unique restaurants (CAMIS).
- Inspection dates run from 2007 to August 2026, but most records are from 2022 to 2026 (only about 2,600 rows are older). Trend and seasonality work should focus on 2022 to 2026.

## Data quality issues to clean
- 3,714 rows have the placeholder date 01/01/1900. These are restaurants that have not been inspected yet. We will drop them.
- 351 rows have BORO = 0.
- 169 rows are exact duplicates.
- GRADE is empty in 150,672 rows and also contains the codes N, Z and P.
- SCORE is missing in 17,226 rows.
- CRITICAL FLAG has a "Not Applicable" value (8,262 rows), used when there are no violations.
- Other missing values: CUISINE DESCRIPTION (3,794), ZIPCODE (3,082), Census Tract (4,742).

## Useful columns
| Purpose | Columns |
|---|---|
| Outcome | SCORE, GRADE, ACTION (closures and re-openings) |
| Risk | VIOLATION CODE, CRITICAL FLAG |
| Text mining | VIOLATION DESCRIPTION |
| Place | BORO, ZIPCODE, NTA, Census Tract, Latitude, Longitude |
| Other | CUISINE DESCRIPTION, INSPECTION TYPE, INSPECTION DATE |

Planned data integration: merge census tract income and demographic data using the Census Tract column.

## Cleaning plan
1. Load the CSV and check shape, types and missing values.
2. Remove duplicates and placeholder dates.
3. Fix dates, BORO = 0, zip codes and text columns.
4. Handle grade codes, scores and critical flags.
5. Build an inspection-level table (one row per inspection).
6. Build a restaurant-level table with compliance indicators.
7. Create features (month, year, chain vs independent, cleaned violation text).
8. Save the cleaned files for analysis.

